import std/io

// THE ABSENT-KEY FAULT TEXT RENDERS A KEY THE WAY `value.Display()` DOES, WHICH
// IS NOT THE WAY `impl Display` DOES.
//
// This fixture is the COUNTEREXAMPLE to the rule the builder used to apply, and
// it is a fact about the language rather than about either implementation, so it
// cannot expire. eval's applyDestructure passes `keyVal.Display()`
// (eval.go:13728) — a method on the VALUE. It does not dispatch to a Nomi
// `impl Display`, so the impl below is NOT consulted:
//
//	interpreter  line N: key Meters(2.5) not found in map
//	builder before this fixture existed
//	             line N: key 2.5m not found in map
//
// `Meters` has an `impl Display` returning `"${v}m"` for exactly that reason: a
// distinct with no impl renders structurally under either rule, so it cannot
// tell the two apart. Only a type whose impl DISAGREES with the structural text
// discriminates, and this is the smallest such program.
//
// THE CORPUS IS STRUCTURALLY BLIND TO THIS. No file in `tests` reaches
// a missing destructuring key over a Display-impl'd named type, so
// `DIFFED = 0` over the whole sweep said nothing about it, in either direction.
// That is the same blindness the maps fixture's header describes: an observable
// rule with one encoding on each side and no program comparing them.
//
// THE `hit` LINE IS THE POSITIVE CONTROL and not decoration. Its VALUE is also a
// Meters, so `"hit = ${hit}"` prints `9.5m` — string interpolation IS
// `impl Display`, so this line proves the impl is live and reachable in this
// very program. Without it the fixture is consistent with the impl being dead,
// in which case the miss line below would agree under either rule and the
// comparison would establish nothing. A destructuring that HITS also never
// reaches the report, so the partial stdout is what proves the machinery ran.
type Meters Float

impl Display for Meters {
    fn to_string(m: Meters): String {
        case m {
            Meters(v) -> "${v}m"
        }
    }
}

fn main() {
    m = {Meters(1.5) => Meters(9.5)}
    {Meters(1.5) => hit} = m
    io.print("hit = ${hit}")
    {Meters(2.5) => miss} = m
    io.print("unreachable ${miss}")
}
